px in one action.\nAnother possibility: maybe the player moves 2 tiles per keypress (speed 2). Let me test: press RIGHT again and see where it lands. If it moves to (5,9) (2 more tiles), then it's fixed 2-tile steps. If it stops at (5,8) (1 tile), then maybe it slides until it hits a wall, and the wall is... (5,8)=B, (5,9)=W. Hmm, if it slides and stops before a wall: from (5,7), the next is (5,8)=B, then (5,9)=W wall \u2192 stops at (5,8). That's 1 tile.\nAlso, the fact that the tile the player left turned white is strange. Maybe the tiles are \"footprints\" \u2014 the player paints tiles white? Or the tiles are dynamic: gray tiles turn white after the player passes over them? The tile (5,6) that was passed over is still gray. Hmm, but (5,5) became white.\nActually wait \u2014 maybe the tiles are moving! Like a conveyor or shifting maze. The diff only shows changes in the player region and the HUD. So no.\nLet me think about the \"c\" dot: it was at the center (16,16), now it's at (16,23) \u2014 the right edge of the blue block. Maybe the dot indicates the direction of motion (facing right).\nHypothesis: the player moves 2 tiles per action, and the tiles it passes over/leaves turn white (a trail). Actually, the tile it left turned white, and the tile it entered was already white. The tile it passed over (5,6) is still gray. Hmm, so the trail is only the tile that was left behind? That's strange.\nOr: the maze tiles are \"walls\" that the player can pass through, and the player's trail turns tiles white... I'm confused. Let me probe: press RIGHT again and observe.", "kind": "reasoning", "inContext": true}, {"label": "ASSISTANT", "content": "The player moved 2 tiles right in one action, and its previous tile turned white. Probing another RIGHT to learn the movement rule:", "kind": "reasoning", "inContext": true}, {"label": "TOOL CALL: python", "content": "<tool_call>\n<function=python>\n<parameter=code>\nres = action(['RIGHT'])\nseg = current_frame.segmentation\nfor 